Attributions

Several patterns in this repository were folded in from neighbouring
open-source projects. Folding proven ideas in from elsewhere, with
attribution, is itself a practice worth keeping.

- The typed reference annotation (`x-cac-ref`) generalises the typed PathRef
  idea from CorpoSpec (https://github.com/CorpoSpec/corpospec.com), MIT
  licensed. No CorpoSpec code or schema text is included here.

- The write discipline in docs/standards/write-discipline.md and the
  concurrency conventions in docs/standards/concurrency.md are adapted from
  Organizational Seed (https://github.com/codeyogi911/organizational-seed),
  Apache-2.0 licensed, which distilled them from live operation.

- The repository self-inspection framing behind `cac doctor` comes from the
  same project's `tools/doctor`.

- The separation between operator-owned strategy and agent-invoked patterns,
  and the governance-latency argument for keeping layers apart, are informed
  by CompanyOS (https://github.com/tomevault-io/companyos), MIT licensed.

- The capability fallback-chain pattern generalises the capability ownership
  and fallback model in Company Wizard
  (https://github.com/Yesterday-AI/paperclip-plugin-company-wizard), MIT
  licensed.

- The framing of Company-as-Code as a queryable, versionable, integrated,
  testable, accessible system comes from Daniel Rothmann's "Company as Code"
  (https://blog.42futures.com/p/company-as-code).

Each of these is an idea adopted and reimplemented, not code copied. Where a
document adapts specific wording, it says so and links to the source.
